// An owning reference is a move type when it cannot be aliased, and its
// permission governs writes through it exactly as a borrowed reference's does.
//
// 'move' establishes the five ways a type acquires move semantics and leaves
// this one here: ir/types/reference.c:45 infects a reference type when its
// permission lacks MayAlias or its region is itself a move type. A borrow can
// reach neither -- '&uni' is rejected outright -- so an owning region is the
// only way to build a reference that moves, which makes this the group's.
//
// Every diagnostic is in one function on purpose. Data-flow analysis runs per
// function and only when that function's own type check raised nothing:
// fnDclTypeCheck compares the error count against the one it entered with, so
// a function that raises one reports no further flow diagnostics of its own,
// while a later function still gets its own flow pass.

fn moving() {
  // 'so' is declared 'struct @move' in corelib, so every reference into it is a
  // move type however aliasable its permission: the second read of the source
  // finds nothing there
  imm sole = +so 1
  imm moved = sole
  imm again = sole             //~ ErrorMove "has been moved out"

  // The other path to the same place: an allocation with no stated permission is
  // 'uni', which lacks MayAlias, so an 'rc' reference moves too even though the
  // region counts references
  imm unique = +rc 2
  imm taken = unique
  imm twice = unique           //~ ErrorMove "has been moved out"

  // For contrast, the aliasable permission on the same region copies, and the
  // source is still there. Two reads of it raise nothing.
  imm shared = +rc-mut 3
  imm copy1 = shared
  imm copy2 = shared

  // The permission belongs to the reference, so a 'ro' allocation may not be
  // written through any more than a 'ro' borrow may
  imm readonly = +rc-ro 4
  *readonly = 5                //~ ErrorNoMut "permission to modify lval"
}
